import{_ as t,o,c as a,a5 as i}from"./chunks/framework.DSg0KOwT.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"First investigation routing","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"contributor-guide/architecture/widget-rendering/investigation-routing.md","filePath":"contributor-guide/architecture/widget-rendering/investigation-routing.md"}'),d={name:"contributor-guide/architecture/widget-rendering/investigation-routing.md"};function s(r,e,n,c,h,l){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i('<h1 id="first-investigation-routing" tabindex="-1">First investigation routing <a class="header-anchor" href="#first-investigation-routing" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;First investigation routing&quot;">​</a></h1><p>How the dashboard routes a reader from a finding to the place to investigate it.</p><p>Routing is decided as data. The board&#39;s layout says which cards exist, in which tab and order, and which rows each card lists. A route is a pure function of a target and that layout. No component looks for another component, waits for a card to mount, or reads the DOM to find where to land.</p><h2 id="targets" tabindex="-1">Targets <a class="header-anchor" href="#targets" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Targets&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The dashboard has a view-only per-finding route, not a single first-investigation pick: every verdict step, every Findings-tab recommendation row (<code>FixTheseFirst.tsx</code>&#39;s row components inside <code>ImpactBoard</code>), the visible-finding control in Stage Summary (<code>StageTable.tsx</code>) and the stage detail dialog each request their own target. <code>detector-registry.tsx</code> owns each routeable finding type&#39;s stable <code>widgetId</code>; its label comes from core <code>FINDING_PRESENTATION</code> (<code>findingName</code>) and its title from the widget itself. Eligible targets have a mapped, routeable registry entry and a non-empty trimmed recommendation (<code>triageTargetFor</code>, <code>src/view/triage-target.ts</code>). The target&#39;s widget still renders every affected stage in its local order.</p><p><code>src/view/triage-target.ts</code> also exports <code>rankTriageTargets</code>, which orders <code>catalog</code> by the run interpretation&#39;s <code>savingsRank</code>. That rank comes from core&#39;s <code>rankBySavings</code> (<code>packages/core/src/run-verdict.ts</code>, shared with the CLI/MCP verdict), which orders every routeable finding by its stage&#39;s task time (<code>impactEstimate.stageTaskMs</code>), then by potential savings (<code>impactEstimate.wallClock.high</code>), a quantified estimate ahead of an unquantified one, then impact band, then widget display order (<code>FINDING_DISPLAY_ORDER</code>, core&#39;s copy of <code>orderedWidgets()</code>, which a <code>tests/view/detector-registry.test.tsx</code> case keeps equal), then catalog order; <code>selectTriageTarget</code> is its first entry. The occupancy-weighted impact estimator gives every finding a comparable <code>impactEstimate.wallClock</code> figure, and ranking by it keeps a &quot;start here&quot; pick off a <code>skew</code>/<code>straggler</code> finding ranked <code>critical</code> on a ratio basis whose occupancy-clipped recoverable time is near zero, when a lower-severity finding beside it has an order-of-magnitude larger recoverable-time estimate. Impact band only breaks ties. <code>RunVerdict</code>&#39;s next steps are built from this ranking.</p><p>A target is not persisted and does not extend the core <code>Finding</code> contract. Rows are keyed by the core <code>findingKey</code> (<code>src/view/finding-keys.ts</code>, <code>runFindingKeys</code>), made unique within the run, so a finding of another run lands on the row that shows the same finding. Cross-session consumers (exports, URL-restored state) use the core <code>Finding.id</code> instead (see <a href="./../worker-protocol/evidence-report.html#finding-identity">Finding identity</a>).</p><p>Beyond a finding, a route can ask for an evidence ledger row (<code>{ kind: &#39;evidence&#39;, key }</code>) or a landmark (<code>{ kind: &#39;landmark&#39;, id }</code>): the verdict, the Findings board, an impact band&#39;s heading or the filter bar.</p><h2 id="the-layout" tabindex="-1">The layout <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-layout" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The layout&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>buildBoardLayout</code> (<code>src/view/board-layout.ts</code>) is a pure function of the run&#39;s interpretation, the finding filter and the widget density. It returns:</p><ul><li><code>steps</code>: the verdict&#39;s steps, each with the evidence it shows in place. A <code>panel</code> step has an evidence panel and offers the control for it; a <code>bare</code> step has nothing beyond its line and is itself the landing; a step with no evidence routes to its widget&#39;s card.</li><li><code>bands</code>: per impact band, the board&#39;s rows (single findings and type groups, each with its evidence panel or none) and the evidence cards no step or row shows in place. A card a step or row already shows is in the band too, listed in its <code>routeOnlyCardKeys</code>: it appears only once a route has revealed it, which is how a route to a finding no step or row holds (a log note, an evidence caveat) still lands.</li><li><code>reference</code>: the Full app report&#39;s tiles the data has content for.</li><li><code>cards</code>: every card by key, with its tab and the keys of the rows it lists.</li><li><code>lines</code>: the controls the triage shortcuts step through, in reading order.</li><li><code>holders</code>: for each finding key, the step or row line that holds it and the widget card that lists it.</li></ul><p>The same layout is what the views render: <code>RunVerdict</code> renders <code>steps</code>, <code>ImpactBoard</code> renders <code>bands</code>, <code>ReferenceSection</code> renders <code>reference</code>. A tile with nothing to show is absent from the layout, so it leaves no blank grid cell, and a widget&#39;s evidence panel exists exactly when the widget&#39;s <code>rows</code> function keeps a row (see <a href="./row-expand-contract.html#in-place-evidence-panel">row-expand-contract</a>). <code>FilteredBoard</code> (<code>Dashboard.tsx</code>) builds the layout once per interpretation, filter and density and provides it through <code>BoardLayoutContext</code>.</p><h2 id="resolving-a-route" tabindex="-1">Resolving a route <a class="header-anchor" href="#resolving-a-route" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Resolving a route&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>resolveRoute(target, layout)</code> returns <code>{ tab, openCards, cardKey, rowKey }</code> or null when the layout has no place for the target (a finding the filter or the run does not list). For a finding:</p><ol><li>A step or row with an evidence panel opens that panel and lands on the finding&#39;s own row inside it.</li><li>A step or row whose widget has nothing beyond it is the landing: the route lands on its label or title.</li><li>Otherwise the widget&#39;s card opens and the route lands on the finding&#39;s row in it, or on the card&#39;s own title when the card lists no row for the finding (a finding the filter hides from an always-mounted card).</li></ol><p><code>tab</code> is the tab that holds the landing, or null for the verdict, which sits above the tabs. The Core Locality card lives in Full app report, so a route to it shows that tab; every other card is in Findings.</p><p><code>routeTo(layout, target)</code> (<code>src/view/board-route.ts</code>) resolves a target and starts the route in the store. A jump from an unfiltered surface (the verdict, the stage dialog, the top bar&#39;s count chip) first clears only the filter dimensions that hide the target, builds the layout for that selection, and resolves against it, so the route and the filter change happen together.</p><h2 id="route-state" tabindex="-1">Route state <a class="header-anchor" href="#route-state" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Route state&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The Findings board&#39;s state for the loaded run is the store&#39;s <code>board</code> slice (<code>src/store/board.ts</code>): the active tab, the open cards, the cards a route has revealed, the expanded group, the active route, the line holding focus, and the widget state described below. It is replaced when a run loads. A route in the store carries its token, whether it flashes its row and where it scrolls to.</p><p>A card&#39;s disclosure is controlled by the board: <code>WidgetCard</code> is open when its key is in <code>openCards</code>, and a route opens the cards its result lists. A card that is not in a layout (a widget rendered on its own, as in the docs examples) keeps its own state.</p><h2 id="landing" tabindex="-1">Landing <a class="header-anchor" href="#landing" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Landing&quot;">​</a></h2><p>An element that can be a landing takes <code>useLanding(keys)</code> (<code>route-landing.ts</code>) as its ref callback. When the active route&#39;s <code>rowKey</code> is one of its keys, the element scrolls itself into view and focuses itself in the commit that mounts it, so a route to a row in a card the route opens, or in a tab the route shows, needs no wait for the card or the tab. Each route lands once: an element that mounts again under the same route does not take focus again, and a newer route to the same place lands again. <code>useAnchoredRow</code> (<code>finding-anchor.ts</code>) gives every row a finding widget renders its landing, its <code>tabIndex={-1}</code> and its flash.</p><p>A flash is the <code>route-flash</code> CSS animation, which ends by itself; the next route or a tab change ends the mark. Only a row that mounts while the route is pending flashes, so a row that mounts again after the landing does not flash again. While a route is pending, a paginated list shows the page that holds the routed row: <code>RowList</code> (<code>finding-widget.tsx</code>) finds the row&#39;s index in its ordered rows during render and passes it to <code>usePagedRows</code>, and a group&#39;s member list (<code>TypeGroupRow</code>) does the same with its member lines. The list stores that page, so after the landing the reader pages from it freely.</p><h2 id="triage-shortcuts" tabindex="-1">Triage shortcuts <a class="header-anchor" href="#triage-shortcuts" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Triage shortcuts&quot;">​</a></h2><p>In Advanced view <code>useTriageShortcuts</code> binds j/k to step through <code>lines</code> from the line that holds focus, <code>f</code> to land on the filter bar and 1/2 to switch tabs. A group&#39;s evidence control is its own line, right after the group&#39;s toggle. A group&#39;s member lines are walked only while the group is expanded (<code>walkableLines</code>). A line reports focus to the store (<code>useLine</code>), and the shortcut starts a route to the next line without a flash.</p><h2 id="widget-state-and-what-is-mounted" tabindex="-1">Widget state and what is mounted <a class="header-anchor" href="#widget-state-and-what-is-mounted" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Widget state and what is mounted&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The board mounts only what is showing. A hidden tab renders none of its widgets, and a board card that is collapsed renders no body (<code>WidgetCard.tsx</code>). A card on its own keeps its collapsed body mounted, hidden, because nothing outside it holds its state. What a widget remembers while its body is unmounted (a page, a sort, the Stage Summary&#39;s filter, the Clean checks disclosure) lives in the <code>board</code> slice through <code>useWidgetState</code> (<code>src/view/useWidgetState.ts</code>), under a key from the card (<code>useCardStateKey</code>) or a fixed one, and goes when the run does.</p><h2 id="guards" tabindex="-1">Guards <a class="header-anchor" href="#guards" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Guards&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>rules/no-dom-routing.yml</code> fails <code>npm run lint:structure</code> for <code>document.getElementById</code>, <code>querySelector</code>, <code>querySelectorAll</code>, <code>getClientRects</code>, <code>offsetParent</code>, <code>requestAnimationFrame</code> and <code>queueMicrotask</code> under <code>src/view/</code> and <code>src/docs-widget/</code>. Measured layout (charts, the plan graph, the Stage Summary scroll hint) and the docs host are allowlisted in the rule; a document this app does not render takes a suppression with its reason.</p><h2 id="tests" tabindex="-1">Tests <a class="header-anchor" href="#tests" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Tests&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><code>tests/view/board-layout.test.ts</code> builds the layout and resolves a route for every finding of every docs example run, in both densities, without rendering.</li><li><code>tests/view/route-focus.test.tsx</code> renders the exported dashboard for each of those runs, routes to each example finding and asserts <code>document.activeElement</code> is a visible element inside the resolved card, with no fake timers.</li><li><code>tests/view/finding-anchor.test.tsx</code> covers a row&#39;s landing, flash and once-per-route rule.</li><li><code>tests/view/board-mounting.test.tsx</code> covers that a hidden tab and a collapsed card hold no widgets and that widget state survives.</li><li><code>tests/view/triage-navigation.test.tsx</code> keeps a few App-level smoke tests of the wiring between the controls, the store and the board.</li></ul><p>This route deliberately does not cover detector/parser or threshold changes, or generic badges, tags, chips, or StagePills as controls. Config Audit findings route like any other: they resolve against <code>catalog</code> ∪ <code>configFindings</code>.</p>',32)])])}const p=t(d,[["render",s]]);export{g as __pageData,p as default};
